When you're paying off several debts at once, the order you attack them in changes how fast you're free — and how it feels along the way.

## Avalanche: highest interest first

Pay minimums on everything, then throw every extra rupee at the debt with the highest interest rate. Mathematically, this minimises total interest paid — it's the cheapest path out.

## Snowball: smallest balance first

Pay minimums on everything, then attack the smallest balance regardless of its rate. It costs slightly more in interest, but clearing a debt entirely — even a small one — builds momentum that keeps people going.

## What we found

On a real budget, avalanche saved more in absolute terms, but the difference was smaller than expected once a debt-free date was in sight either way. The better strategy is the one you'll actually stick to — which is why Salli's payoff planner shows both, side by side, against your real numbers.
